ORDINANCE NO. ________

ENTITLED: “AN ORDINANCE AMENDING, VARIOUS SECTIONS CONTAINED IN
CHAPTERS 8.20, 8.24, 8.28 AND 8.32 WITHIN TITLE 8, HEALTH AND
SAFETY, OF THE CHEYENNE CITY CODE RELATING TO GENERAL
PROVISIONS
PERTAINING
TO
FIRE
PROTECTION
AND
REGULATIONS.

BE IT ORDAINED BY THE GOVERNING BODY OF THE CITY OF CHEYENNE,
WYOMING:

Section 1.  That various sections contained in Chapters 8.20, 8.24, 8.28 and 8.32 within Title
8, Health and Safety, of the Cheyenne City Code, relating to general provisions pertaining to fire
protection and regulations  are hereby amended as set out in this ordinance.  Chapters and
sections currently existing within Title 8, Health and Safety, not set out within this ordinance
remain unchanged.

Section 2.  That Chapter 8.20, Fireworks, is amended as follows:

8.20.050
Public displays – Licensing conditions.

A.  Notwithstanding the provisions of Sections 8.20.010 and 8.20.020 of this chapter, a
license may be granted for supervised public displays of fireworks by fair associations,
amusement parks and other organizations or group of individuals.  Licenses may be granted upon
application to the city clerk after approval of the police and fire authorities, the filing of a bond
by the applicant as required by this section, and payment of the license fee in the amount of one
hundred fifteen dollars ($115.00) per event or activity.

C.  An applicant for a license must furnish a bond or certificate of insurance in an amount not
less than three hundred thousand dollars ($300,000.00) public liability and one hundred thousand
dollars ($100,000.00) property damage for the payment of all damages that may be caused either
to a person or persons or to property by reason of the licensed display, and arising from any acts
of the licensee, his or her agents, employees or subcontractors.

C.  AN APPLICANT FOR A LICENSE SHALL FILE WITH THE CITY CLERK A
CERTIFICATE OF COMMERCIAL GENERAL LIABILITY INSURANCE IN A
MINIMAL
AMOUNT
OF
$1,000,000
PER
OCCURRENCE
AND
$2,000,000
AGGREGATE OR IN AN AMOUNT AS REQUIRED BY THE CITY RISK MANAGER
OR CITY ATTORNEY, INDEMNIFYING THE PUBLIC AGAINST DAMAGES
SUSTAINED EITHER TO A PERSON OR PERSONS OR TO PROPERTY BY REASON
OF THE LICENSED DISPLAY, AND ARISING FROM ANY ACTS OF THE
LICENSEE, HIS OR HER AGENTS, EMPLOYEES OR SUBCONTRACTORS.  IN
ADDITION IF THE DISPLAY IS ON PROPERTY OWNED BY THE CITY OF
CHEYENNE, THE CERTIFICATE OF INSURANCE WILL LIST THE CITY OF
CHEYENNE AS CERTIFICATE HOLDER AND ADDITIONAL INSURED BY
ENDORSEMENT.
```

### Page 2 of 5

```text
Section 3.  That Chapter 8.24, Flammable and Combustible Liquids, is amended as follows:

8.24.010
Codes adopted by reference.

Codes promulgated by the National Fire Code Volumes NFPA 1 CHAPTERS 2, 3 and 13,
NATIONAL FIRE PROTECTION ASSOCIATION PAMPHLET NO. 30, National Fire
Protection Association Pamphlet No. 385, and National Fire Protection Association Pamphlet
No. 329, and their appendices and the latest edition of the Uniform INTERNATIONAL Fire
Code as adopted by other ordinances PURSUANT TO CHEYENNE CITY CODE,
CHAPTER 8.12, are adopted and made a part of this chapter involving flammable and
combustible liquids.

8.24.020
Aboveground tanks in city limits.

It is unlawful to have aboveground storage tanks exceeding sixty (60) gallons except in
refinery complexes and bulk plants, which shall be in heavy industrial (HI) zone areas only;
except, however, that aboveground storage tanks exceeding sixty (60) gallons may be permitted
in a HEAVY INDUSTRIAL (HI), light industrial (LI) or public (P) zone with the approval of,
and under conditions established by, the chief of the Cheyenne fire department.  Such tanks
exceeding sixty (60) gallons in refinery complexes and bulk plants shall be in compliance with
the National Fire Protection Association Pamphlet No. 30 (1993 Edition), and the Uniform
INTERNATIONAL Fire Code.

8.24.060
Tank system tightness testing – Requirements.

D.  It is the duty of the operators EVERY OPERATOR OF ANY TANK CONTAINING
of flammable and OR combustible liquids tanks or tanks, and OR related equipment, to SHALL
maintain daily records of flammable and combustible liquids stock, as proposed in the
CURRENT EDITION OF American Petroleum Institute Publication RECOMMENDED
PRACTICE NO. 1621,  Third Edition, 1977, entitled "Recommended Practice for Bulk Liquid
Stock Control at Outlets." "BULK LIQUID STOCK CONTROL AT RETAIL OUTLETS."

E.  When any EVERY user OF ANY TANK CONTAINING ANY flammable OR and
combustible liquids tanks and OR related equipment WHO notices a loss of product, or is
suspicious of a loss, or NOTICES THE presence of water is evident in storage ANY SUCH
tank or tanks, SHALL ADHERE TO procedures from THE National Fire Protection
Association Pamphlet No. 329 and the recommended practices of liquid stock control outlined by
THE CURRENT EDITION OF American Petroleum Institute Publication RECOMMENDED
PRACTICE Number NO. 1621, Third Edition, 1977.  shall be adhered to.  Any loss of product,
or suspicion of a loss, or presence of water is evident shall be reported immediately to the fire
department, fire prevention bureau FIRE PREVENTION BUREAU OF THE FIRE
DEPARTMENT.
```

